Our central finding is that the value of formal verification depends on the \emph{independence} of its inputs, not the soundness of its logic. We distinguish two levels of generality. \emph{SICA-specific}: under source-attribution scoring and empirical degeneration, SICA reduces to majority vote (Observation~1), and 27 training-free variants fail to escape this collapse. \emph{General}: the DPI bound (Remark~1) applies to any deterministic self-extraction pipeline, and symbolic confirmation bias (BR $> 1$) holds across nine models from 7B to frontier scale, suggesting the bottleneck is intrinsic to self-referential constraint generation. Three diagnostics ($K_\text{eff}$, BR, $C_0$) operationalize this principle and correctly characterize failure modes across all 60 tested conditions, with decision boundaries generalizing prospectively to held-out model families (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic-framework}).

\looseness=-1
This principle has direct consequences for test-time compute scaling \citep{snell2025scaling}: with $K_\text{eff} \leq 3.62$ for $K{=}12$, same-model traces scale redundancy, not information, advancing beyond \citet{huang2024selfcorrect}'s empirical findings to formal self-verification. Escaping the bottleneck requires structural independence and capability ordering (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}); whether trained extractors can shift this boundary, and how cross-architecture verification scales to stronger generators, remain open.

